WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
With everything you've learned, you're just about ready to tackle your first programming assignment.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.160
Before you do that, let me just give you a quick tour of IPython notebooks in Coursera.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.520
Here's the Jupyter IPython notebook that you can get to on Coursera.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.240
Let me just quickly show you a few features of this.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.880
The instructions are written right here in the text in the IPython notebook.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.320
And these little, you know, like gray blocks are blocks of code.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.960
So occasionally, you see in these blocks something that looks like this.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.320
There's a style code here and an end code here.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.560
To do the programming exercise, please make sure to write your code between the style code

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:36.840
here and end code here.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.840
So for example, print hello world.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.480
And then to execute a code block, you can hit shift enter.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.400
And then to execute this code block, which I guess we just wrote print hello world.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.760
So that printed hello world.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.960
To run a cell, you can also, to run one of these code blocks in a cell, you can also

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.440
click cell and then run cell.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:07.240
So to execute this, it's possible that on your computer, the keyboard shortcut for cell

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:13.120
run cell might be different than shift enter, but it's on both my Mac as well as on my

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.520
PC as shift enter.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.880
So might be the same for you as well.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.560
Now when you're reading the instructions, if you, you know, accidentally double click

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.480
on it, you will end up with this markdown language.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.320
If you end up with this funny looking text to convert it back to the nice looking text,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.320
just run the cell.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.680
So you either go to cell run cell, or I'm going to hit shift enter.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.400
And that basically executes the markdown and turns it back into this nice looking code.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.960
Just a couple more tips.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:49.440
When you execute code like this, it actually runs on a kernel on a piece of code that

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.400
runs on a server.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.600
If you're running an excessively large job or if you leave a computer for a very long

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.840
time or something goes wrong, your internet connection or something, there is a small

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.240
chance that the kernel on the back end might die, in which case just click kernel

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.960
and then restart kernel and hopefully that will reboot the kernel and make it work

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.960
again.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.760
So that shouldn't happen if you're just running relatively small jobs and you just

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.520
started up the IPython notebook.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.360
But if you see an error message that the kernel has died or something, you can

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.480
try kernel restart.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.000
Finally in an IPython notebook like this, there may be multiple blocks of code.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.280
So even if an earlier block of code doesn't have any greater code, be sure to execute

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.800
this block of code because in this example, it imports NumPy as np and so on and

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.960
sets up some of the variables that you might need in order to execute the lower

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.720
down blocks of code.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.680
So be sure to execute the ones on top even if you aren't asked to write any code

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.280
in them.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.360
And finally when you're done implementing your solutions, there's this blue

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.680
submit assignment button here on the upper right and we click that and we'll

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.600
submit your solutions for grading.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:07.400
I found that the interactive command shell nature of IPython notebooks to be

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.360
very useful for letting you quickly implement a few lines of code, see an outcome,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.520
learn and iterate quickly.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.440
And so I hope that the program exercises in the Coursera Jupyter IPython

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.920
notebooks will help you quickly learn and experiment and see how to implement

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.800
these learning algorithms.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.720
There's one more video after this, this is an optional video that talks about

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.600
the cost function for logistic regression.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.240
You can watch that or not.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.960
Either way is perfectly fine.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:39.120
Either way, best of luck with the week two programming assignment and I also look

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.720
forward to seeing you at the start of the weekly materials.
